from pathlib import Path
import csv,json,re,datetime,hashlib
D=Path('TOHOKU_CHECK');p=D/'CHECK_EVENTS.tsv';backup=D/'CHECK_EVENTS.before-notes-normalization.tsv'
assert not backup.exists();backup.write_bytes(p.read_bytes())
rows=list(csv.DictReader(open(p),delimiter='\t'));fields=list(rows[0]);now=datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S UTC');who=rows[0]['who']
CANON='refuted, with the refined statement\'s id'
MAP={'proof check':'targeted proof check','independent example':'independent re-proof','direct derivation':'independent re-proof','comparison construction and direction test':CANON,'direct proof':'independent re-proof','counterexample and corrected proof':CANON}
changes=[]
for n,r in enumerate(rows,1):
 old=r['type'];new=CANON if old.startswith('refuted,') else MAP.get(old,old)
 if old!=new:
  if old.startswith('refuted,'):
   m=re.search(r'(?:TOH[-:]|TOH\d{3}[-:]|p\d{3}[-:])[A-Za-z0-9_.:–-]+',old)
   if m and m.group() not in r['outcome']:r['outcome']='Refined statement '+m.group()+'. '+r['outcome']
  r['type']=new;changes.append({'event_record':n,'statement_id':r['statement_id'],'old_type':old,'new_type':new,'reason':'Primary reread of the complete outcome; canonical user vocabulary records the actual proof, example, direction test or refutation. No strengthening of the mathematical outcome.'})
 if r['statement_id']=='p210-corollary-closed-cover':r['outcome']='Refined statement p210-corollary-closed-cover-refined. '+r['outcome']
 if r['statement_id']=='p211-remark-Ib':r['outcome']='Refined statement p211-remark-Ib-corrected. '+r['outcome']
def add(s,t,o):
 rows.append(dict(statement_id=s,date=now,who=who,type=t,outcome=o))
add('TOH-N-P134-wellpowered-pullbacks','independent re-proof','Refined statement: a locally small category with pullbacks and a set of generators in the printed proper-subobject sense is well-powered. Associate to each subobject B of A the set of all generator maps into A that factor through B. If B and C give the same set, their pullback P maps monomorphically to B. If that map were proper, a generator map into B not factoring through P would, by equality of the two sets, factor through C and hence P, contradiction. The same holds for C, so B=C as subobjects. The collection embeds in a power set of a set.')
add('TOH-N-P135-free-diagram-generators','targeted proof check','Refined statement under smallness and the needed coproduct/cokernel hypotheses: use the free diagram left adjoint to evaluation, not the supported-at-a-vertex diagram. Form coproducts of A indexed by paths, then impose the linear path relations by cokernels; maps out are exactly maps from A into the evaluated diagram. Coproducts of these free diagrams on a generator detect proper subdiagrams. For a poset the explicit exact formula and its injectivity consequence are independently proved in TOH-N-P169-diagram-repair. General relation quotients need not make the left adjoint exact.')
add('TOH-N-P141-variance','targeted proof check','Refined statement: dualize on the source for contravariant boundary functors, replacing the covariant monomorphism-effacement condition by the corresponding epimorphism condition. Naturality then gives the same cokernel comparison proof in the opposite category. The explicit Ext^1(-,Z) counterexample recorded under TOH-P141-P2.2.1-variance rules out unqualified use of monomorphisms.')
add('TOH-N-P142-negative-coeffaceability','targeted proof check','Refined statement: in the dual satellite theorem, strictly negative satellites are coeffaceable; degree zero remains the prescribed F and is not included. This is the exact opposite-category counterpart of positive-degree effaceability. The identity-functor example in TOH-P142-dual-coeffaceability checks necessity of the strict inequality; the full satellite-existence proof remains a cited background dependency.')
add('TOH-N-P142-halfwise-universality','carried as background without fault found','Refined statement: the positive and negative satellite constructions have their respective one-sided universal properties with the prescribed degree-zero functor. A stitched exact boundary functor is not asserted to be initial among all two-sided boundary functors. The nonuniqueness calculation is checked in TOH-P142-MATH-001; the full one-sided existence theorem is not newly reproved here.')
add('TOH-N-P144-projectives-sheaves','targeted proof check','Refined statement: general sheaf categories need not have enough projectives; this is not an assertion that none ever do. Over one point the category is a module category, and a free module on the underlying set gives a projective epimorphism onto every module. The negative existence phenomenon for other sheaf categories is carried as background, not established by this one-point check.')
add('TOH-N-P145-filtration-bound','targeted proof check','Refined filtration bound: for the first-quadrant convention, F^mE^n=0 for m>n, while F^nE^n may contain the final graded piece. This follows by exhausting the finite filtration with graded pieces indexed 0 through n. No assertion F^nE^n=0 is warranted.')
add('TOH-N-P159-product-nonempty','targeted proof check','Refined flasque-product statement: assume each set factor has a selected element, or adjoin a base point first. A section over an open subset is an assignment on its points; extend it by the chosen elements elsewhere. Every sheaf maps monomorphically into the product of its pointed stalk sets since its germs are detected by their own coordinates. This proves flasqueness; it does not by itself supply a general closed-subset softness proof.')
add('TOH-4.1-center-sheaf-action','independent re-proof','Refined statement: global sheaf Hom is a module over Gamma(X,O^natural), and internal Hom over the sheaf center O^natural. Such a scalar commutes with every ring section on every smaller open, so multiplication on an O-linear map remains O-linear and respects restrictions. The Sierpinski example under TOH-4.1-global-center-action shows why the center of Gamma(X,O) alone is insufficient.')
add('TOH-4.2-open-subset-restriction','independent re-proof','Refined statement: on open subsets, restriction is exact and preserves injective module sheaves because its extension-by-zero left adjoint is exact. Restricting an injective resolution therefore computes local Ext and the indicated natural restriction maps. For arbitrary subspaces, Ext can be defined separately but this resolution argument and the internal-Hom restriction identity need not apply.')
add('p194:group-ring-injective','independent re-proof','Refined statement: the global sections of an injective k[G]-module sheaf form an injective k[G]-module, by the exact constant-sheaf left adjoint. Restriction of scalars to Z[G] preserves injectivity here because k[G] is flat over Z[G] only when k is Z-flat, so that shortcut is not used. Instead for the cohomology comparison use the appropriate derived-invariants acyclicity via the split bar resolution; k[G]-injectivity makes Hom from the bar resolution of k exact, hence H^n(G,-)=0 for n>0.')
add('TOH-p206-cor-finiteness-parenthesis-refined','targeted proof check','Refined statement: if X0 is nonempty and G acts trivially on it under (D), choose x in X0; then G=G_x is finite. If X0 is empty, no group-finiteness conclusion follows, while the supported terms vanish and the free-action comparison supplies the corollary as recorded in TOH-p206-cor5.4.1.')
add('TOH-p206-fixed-locus-closedness-refined','targeted proof check','Refined statement: require the fixed-point set to be closed, for example by assuming X Hausdorff. For each g its fixed set is the inverse image of the closed diagonal under x maps to (x,gx); intersections are closed. For a finite prime-order group the nonfree locus is this fixed set. Its invariant closed image in the orbit quotient is closed by the quotient topology.')
add('p215:5.6.1:correct-factor','independent re-proof','Refined statement: if B is constructed as the product of point-supported sheaves with chosen modules B_x, the factors for internal Hom(A,B) are Hom_(O(x))(A(x),B_x). The point-stalk/right-adjoint Hom identity and preservation of products by Hom give this formula. The full stalk B(x) is not the chosen factor B_x; the Sierpinski calculation in p215:5.6.1:factor-versus-stalk verifies the distinction.')
add('p210-corollary-closed-cover-refined','targeted proof check','Refined statement: in the paracompact closed-cover alternative add local finiteness, or use the finite closed-cover alternative separately. Those hypotheses make the sheaf Cech augmentation a resolution and justify the derived section comparison of p209-corollary-2-5.5.3. Combining acyclic intersections with Proposition5.5.4 then proves the corollary; an arbitrary closed singleton cover does not.')
add('p211-remark-Ib-corrected','targeted proof check','Refined statement: the canonical comparison runs from the second spectral sequence of Theorem5.2.1 to that of Theorem5.5.3. Restrict a global injective-resolution section into the augmented cover complex, or refine the free repeated cover (gX) toward the chosen free cover. Equivariant refinement homotopies show independence. The filtered circle example in p211-remark-Ib excludes the reverse direction over the identity abutment.')
add('TOH-P141-P2.2.1-variance',CANON,'Refined statement TOH-N-P141-variance. The previously checked Ext^1(-,Z) character argument refutes the covariant monomorphism-effacement criterion when applied unchanged to a contravariant functor; this event isolates the refutation already present in the earlier targeted check.')
add('TOH-P142-MATH-001',CANON,'Refined statement TOH-N-P142-halfwise-universality. The two maps to the boundary functor concentrated in degree minus one have identical degree-zero components and different degree-minus-one components, as checked earlier, so the asserted all-degree initial universal property fails.')
add('TOH-p207-lemma5.5.2-missing-hypothesis',CANON,'Refined statement TOH-p208-lemma5.5.2-refined. The previously checked counterexample with F(A)=A[2], G(A)=A tensor Z/2 and a degree-zero complex makes the claimed spectral sequence zero while its required abutment is nonzero. The inherited left-exactness of G repairs the obstruction.')
add('TOH220-5.7.7-bis-unrestricted',CANON,'Refined statement TOH220-5.7.7-conditional. The explicit injective product-sheaf example in TOH220-support-injectives-obstruction gives H^1(G,Gamma_Phi B) nonzero whereas derived Gamma_Phi^G on B is zero. Thus unrestricted interchange fails; the sufficient cases in TOH220-5.7.7-conditional are conditional on the separately flagged support comparison.')
add('TOH-P148-remark2-positive-acyclicity-only',CANON,'Refined statement TOH-P148-remark2-qualified. For F(A)=A/2A on Ab and G=identity, F kills injectives, so R^qF=0 for all q including zero. Every object is acyclic in the positive-degree-only sense used in the shorthand. The resolution (Z/2)[0] gives hypercohomology F(Z/2)=Z/2, but the actual derived composite is zero. This refutes that interpretation of Remark2, not Theorem2.4.1 with its injective resolution.')
add('TOH-P148-remark2-qualified','targeted proof check','Sufficient refined statement: assume F left exact, or require each chosen resolution term C^i to have its canonical F(C^i) to RF(C^i) comparison an isomorphism, including degree zero. Then the acyclic-resolution comparison identifies the image complex with RF(A), and applying RG gives the stated hypercohomology spectral sequence. The need for the degree-zero condition was independently checked by the quotient-by-two example; the standard acyclic-resolution theorem under its full hypotheses is used as background.')
add('TOH-P132-ring-diagram-relations','targeted proof check','Reread the additive path-relation definition on pp130–131 and the unwritten ring relations on p132. The permitted integral linear combinations encode r(u+v)-r(u)-r(v)=0 as well as multiplicativity and the unit. Thus the earlier draft suspicion about missing expressibility of additivity was withdrawn before final notes. Writing these relations gives exactly unital ring actions and their commuting morphisms; no original-text correction is indicated.')
allowed={'targeted proof check','independent re-proof','computation or formal certificate','used and rechecked in this run','carried as background without fault found',CANON}
assert all(r['type'] in allowed for r in rows)
with p.open('w',newline='') as f:w=csv.DictWriter(f,fields,delimiter='\t');w.writeheader();w.writerows(rows)
(D/'CHECK_EVENTS_TYPE_REVIEW.json').write_text(json.dumps({'reviewed_utc':now,'reviewer':who,'original_rows':187,'canonical_rows_before_note_links':len(rows),'changes':changes,'rule':'Six literal user types; a prior detailed outcome remains evidence only for the work it actually reports. New rows make previously implicit refined-ID bindings and refutations explicit.'},ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print({'normalized_rows':len(rows),'type_changes':len(changes),'added':len(rows)-187})
